#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079246} :activity/id #uuid "e58c72b3-2e84-5709-829e-3b86826cec82" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049794 :topic/id #uuid "1461d069-b2db-522e-b541-a8f313a43937" :topic/math-academy-id 610} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079640 :tutorial/content "Using a calculator, we can evaluate sine for the angle of $20^\\circ$, and get\n\n$$\n\\sin(20^{\\circ}) \\approx 0.342\n$$\n\nBut what if we are given the value $0.342$, and we want to find the angle whose sine is this value? To do this, we can use the **inverse trigonometric functions**.\n\nIn the case of the sine, its inverse is **arcsine** (written as $\\arcsin$ or $\\sin^{-1}$). If applied to the sine, it gives the value of the angle:\n\n$$\n\\arcsin (0.342) \\approx 20^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nThe inverses of the other trigonometric functions work in the same way.\n\n- The inverse of sine is $\\arcsin$.\n- The inverse of cosine is $\\arccos$.\n- The inverse of tangent is $\\arctan$.\n\nTry it for yourself. But make sure that your calculator is in \"degrees\" mode!\n\n**Note:** In this topic, we only deal with acute angles. Things get a little more complicated for non-acute angles, but you'll learn about that later!" :tutorial/id #uuid "259189af-6378-518d-a123-d831688aed97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2450 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f93cf90c-35eb-5ffe-884d-35a89ebf6a8b" :step/math-academy-id 10975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078395 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078259 :question/id #uuid "200cec1b-b266-557f-a2ef-db02cf6d941a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3251"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e4cc25e-b0e9-5935-86f7-c450c990cef8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078841}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "148b95eb-c3f7-5f1b-881e-5dc27b007df7" :step/math-academy-id 10976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079259}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078396 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078260 :question/id #uuid "01ba80ea-e3d7-5372-9732-d721f039bae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3252"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81772ee2-6b04-559c-85a3-976fd12bbd74" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078843}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Angles Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "9ac21fb4-7047-5b4d-879f-08f183f19294" :step/math-academy-id 10977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079641 :tutorial/content "The inverse trigonometric functions are particularly useful when we need to find the measure of an angle in a right triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the measure of the angle $\\theta$ indicated in the triangle below.\n\n![](images/85/85c384f5cbfc9d9bfa4ffebeafc3dbe66eb756e71f4790b0d9566ebdab559f9c.png)\n\nSince we are given the length of the hypotenuse and opposite sides to $\\theta$, we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin (\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} &= \\dfrac{5}{7}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin (\\theta) &= \\dfrac{5}{7} \\\\[5pt] \\arcsin(\\sin (\\theta)) &= \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7} \\right) \\\\[5pt] \\theta &= \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=(45.584\\,6...)^\\circ \\\\[5pt] &\\approx 46^\\circ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a55899c-f08a-5577-a1de-9fb483947ddf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2451 :tutorial/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle in a Right Triangle Using Trigonometry"} :step/id #uuid "ffeda555-fc7d-5cb3-ba37-a29037e0cba3" :step/math-academy-id 10978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078261 :question/id #uuid "7a4aa28f-8a0b-556f-b6ce-beb674cd0234" :question/math-academy-id "e-3253"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8ccd79e-56a6-58fc-917c-8dc660ad2425" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049915}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078739}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given the Lengths of Two Sides of a Right Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "1b2e5432-c30a-5106-99bc-487755ce72bf" :step/math-academy-id 10979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079648 :tutorial/content "If we have a trigonometric equation like\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand we want to solve for $\\theta$, we can apply the corresponding inverse trigonometric function to both sides of the equation to \"cancel\" out the trigonometric function.\n\nHere, the trigonometric function is $\\cos$, so the inverse trigonometric function is $\\arccos$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arccos (\\cos θ) &= \\arccos (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\nθ &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9953e2ac-f857-5760-a8b2-c5c56367a048" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2560 :tutorial/title "Applying an Inverse Trigonometric Function to Both Sides of an Equation"} :step/id #uuid "bd5b2322-661e-5aa8-9d7a-05b0dd05892e" :step/math-academy-id 11738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079248}}] :activity/title "Calculating Angles in Right Triangles Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078640}]}
